.task-board/.resources/TASK-260715-1fv4z1/TASK-260715-1fv4z1_spawn-log_-analyst--researcher--codex-.log:3568:.task-board/EPIC-260715-2qzczm_resilience-and-performance/STORY-260715-1zzt0c_windows-rekey-and-memory-controls/TASK-260715-1pn983_record-memory-window-rekey-contract/README.md:7:In scope: physical-footprint engineering target and device variance; packet socket buffers; HEV sessions, task stacks, TCP buffers, caches, and queues; SSH lanes, channels, advertised windows, pending writes, and key exchange; DNS and relay buffers; metrics; old and new reconnect transports; ledger units and ownership; reservation and release; soft, pressure, and critical states; action order; sampling semantics; typed refusal and stop; diagrams; M0 through M3 interfaces. Out of scope: claiming Apple memory guarantees, treating all advertised credit as allocated, implementation, final watermark numbers without evidence, jetsam-driven control, payload capture, or weakening safety gates.
.task-board/.resources/TASK-260715-1fv4z1/TASK-260715-1fv4z1_spawn-log_-analyst--researcher--codex-.log:3569:.task-board/EPIC-260715-2qzczm_resilience-and-performance/STORY-260715-21c23x_quic-and-route-policies/README.md:10:1. Allow QUIC forwards eligible UDP/443 only through a current validated relay generation, Block UDP/443 returns the documented fast local failure, and neither mode silently times out or affects unrelated UDP or tunnel DNS. 2. Auto uses versioned lane-health inputs, deterministic thresholds and hysteresis, rejects new UDP/443 quickly when QUIC-over-SSH is predicted to underperform, and records only aggregate reasons. 3. Full, degraded, reasserting, failed, stopping, and stale-generation states each have one explicit UDP/443 outcome with no physical socket fallback. 4. Compatible mode installs dual-stack defaults and only approved exact exclusions, while fail-closed mode enables includeAllNetworks only where supported and exposes the documented system exceptions without absolute claims. 5. Startup, live mode change, endpoint replacement, reconnect, settings failure, and stop apply or roll back route and DNS settings without a recursive route, broad exclusion, false-connected snapshot, or ordinary DNS leak. 6. Unit, fault, and golden tests cover policy decisions, fast-failure latency, unrelated traffic, platform branches, exact routes, rollback, and redaction. 7. Physical iPhone and Mac captures across compatible and fail-closed modes and full, degraded, and reasserting capabilities match the disclosed traffic matrix and record OS behavior changes.
.task-board/.resources/TASK-260715-1fv4z1/TASK-260715-1fv4z1_spawn-log_-analyst--researcher--codex-.log:3576:.task-board/EPIC-260715-21g2pi_product-experience-and-security/STORY-260715-n5dt84_onboarding-accessibility-and-migration/README.md:10:1. A first-time user on iOS and macOS can understand the self-hosted SSH model, complete or resume prerequisites, create a profile, trust a host, acknowledge privacy terms, grant VPN permission, connect, understand capability or failure, obtain help, and disconnect. 2. Privacy disclosure appears before the first enable action and onboarding does not claim anonymity, a Relux exit network, or capabilities not present in the selected mode. 3. All M4 surfaces have stable shared accessibility identifiers, meaningful labels/hints/values, logical focus, keyboard operation on macOS, VoiceOver operation, supported text scaling, sufficient contrast, and reduced-motion behavior. 4. User-facing strings are externalized, pseudo-localization and text expansion pass, and the approved launch-locale policy and fallback are documented. 5. A reviewed decision selects legacy SOCKS coexistence, replacement, or retirement and defines handling of host/account/port defaults, existing release history, bundle/storage identity, rollback, and user messaging before migration code runs. 6. Automated UI journeys plus named physical iPhone and Apple-silicon Mac acceptance cover first run, relaunch/resume, permission denial, empty/error recovery, migration path, full/degraded/failure states, diagnostics, accessibility, and screenshot review.
.task-board/.resources/TASK-260715-1fv4z1/TASK-260715-1fv4z1_spawn-log_-analyst--researcher--codex-.log:3577:.task-board/EPIC-260715-2qzczm_resilience-and-performance/STORY-260715-19ii11_performance-and-resource-tuning/TASK-260715-yjpk5a_implement-approved-minimal-hev-fork/README.md:7:In scope after positive gate only: exact approved upstream commit; minimal callback ingress and egress seam; preserved IPv4 and IPv6 family semantics; bounded ownership and lifetime; EAGAIN or ENOBUFS, EMSGSIZE, cancellation, and cleanup behavior; existing socket-pair fallback or rollback as approved; licenses and notices; patch documentation; rebase strategy; unit, fuzz, integration, physical, and performance comparison. Out of scope: work before gate approval, private APIs, utun descriptor discovery, unrelated HEV changes, protocol or SOCKS behavior changes, speculative tuning, or accepting speed without correctness and memory gates.
.task-board/.resources/TASK-260715-1fv4z1/TASK-260715-1fv4z1_spawn-log_-analyst--researcher--codex-.log:3578:.task-board/.resources/TASK-260715-1loqwb/TASK-260715-1loqwb_hev-udp-handoff.md:3:Consume the accepted outcome from TASK-260715-1vv52g before production adapter integration. The handoff must confirm unmodified HEV, the private SOCKS boundary, and the exact socks5.udp tcp configuration. Do not infer a different HEV framing mode or expose a public proxy.
.task-board/.resources/TASK-260715-1fv4z1/TASK-260715-1fv4z1_spawn-log_-analyst--researcher--codex-.log:3584:.task-board/EPIC-260715-2qzczm_resilience-and-performance/STORY-260715-19ii11_performance-and-resource-tuning/README.md:10:1. A task-scoped protocol defines reproducible workloads, network impairments, warmup, duration, samples, statistical comparison, pass or fail rules, metadata, raw artifact references, redaction, and regression thresholds. 2. Low-overhead bounded instrumentation reconciles packet, transport, DNS, relay, lane, memory, CPU, energy, error, and cleanup counters without payloads, destinations, query names, or high-cardinality identifiers. 3. Untuned iPhone and Mac baselines cover required MTUs, lane counts, window profiles, traffic shapes, concurrency stages, loss and latency, lifecycle, and the provisional HEV configuration. 4. Each accepted parameter change has before-and-after evidence and preserves byte correctness, latency bounds, route safety, zero ordinary DNS fallback, bounded queues, and the extension memory ceiling. 5. The 25 to 30 MiB steady-state target is reported as measured engineering evidence rather than an Apple guarantee, with peak, reconnect overlap, available-memory samples, and any device variance. 6. A HEV fork is approved only if Instruments identifies bridge copies or syscall rate as a material bottleneck and a minimal upstream-trackable change demonstrates meaningful improvement with regression coverage. 7. The final named-device matrix passes lane congestion, rekey, memory pressure, path changes, NAT64, sleep and wake, captive, QUIC, and both route modes or records explicit red rows and blocking follow-up work, and publishes accepted baselines and thresholds.
